          "sample_b1": "In recent years, <strong>deforestation</strong> has become a growing concern in many parts of the world. This problem affects both individuals and society in different ways. <strong>This essay will discuss its main effects.</strong>\n\nOne major effect is that <strong><em>deforestation causes serious damage to the environment and biodiversity</em></strong>. In fact, many forests are natural habitats for animals and plants, so cutting down trees destroys their living environment. In addition, some species may gradually disappear because they cannot survive without forests or find enough food and shelter. As a result, ecosystems become weaker and biodiversity is reduced in many areas around the world.\n\nAnother serious impact is that <strong><em>deforestation contributes to climate change and natural disasters</em></strong>. The main reason is that trees help absorb carbon dioxide and control temperatures in the environment. However, when forests are destroyed, more greenhouse gases remain in the atmosphere and global warming becomes more serious. Consequently, many countries may experience floods, droughts, storms, and soil erosion more frequently than before.\n\nA further consequence is that <strong><em>deforestation negatively affects human life</em></strong>. This is because many local people depend on forests for food, water, medicine, and income in their daily lives. Moreover, environmental damage caused by deforestation can reduce farming productivity and worsen living conditions in these areas. Therefore, many people may face financial difficulties, unemployment, and health problems in the future.\n\nIn conclusion, <strong>deforestation</strong> can lead to several negative effects on both individuals and society. These effects can influence different areas of life. Therefore, governments and citizens should work together to protect forests and plant more trees in the future.",
          "sample_b2": "In recent years, <strong>deforestation</strong> has become a growing environmental concern in many parts of the world. The rapid clearing of forests for agriculture, logging, and urban expansion has created profound challenges for both nature and human society. <strong>This essay will examine the main negative effects of this problem.</strong>\n\nOne major effect is that <strong><em>deforestation causes severe ecological degradation and loss of biodiversity</em></strong>. Forests serve as vital natural habitats for millions of plant and animal species, so large-scale tree clearing directly destroys their living environment. Without adequate shelter and food sources, numerous vulnerable species face the threat of extinction. Consequently, natural food chains are disrupted and global biodiversity is significantly reduced.\n\nAnother serious impact is that <strong><em>forest destruction accelerates climate change and intensifies natural disasters</em></strong>. Trees play a crucial role in absorbing carbon dioxide from the atmosphere and stabilizing the global climate. When vast areas of forest are burned or cut down, massive amounts of greenhouse gases remain trapped in the air, exacerbating global warming. Furthermore, the absence of tree roots weakens soil structure, leading to severe soil erosion, flash floods, landslides, and prolonged droughts across many regions.\n\nA further consequence is that <strong><em>deforestation directly threatens human livelihoods and living standards</em></strong>. Millions of indigenous and rural residents rely on forest resources for freshwater, timber, traditional medicine, and daily income. Moreover, the disruption of local water cycles and soil fertility reduces agricultural productivity, pushing farming communities into economic hardship, unemployment, and food insecurity in the long term.\n\nIn conclusion, <strong>deforestation</strong> leads to devastating consequences, including biodiversity loss, accelerated climate change, and economic hardship for local communities. Addressing this crisis through strict forest conservation and reforestation programs is essential for sustainable development.",
